Exsercise 7:
class Demo {

    int x = 10;
    static int y = 20;

    static void display() {

        System.out.println(x);
        System.out.println(y);

    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        display();

    }
}
OUTPUT : A static method belongs to the class and can directly access only static members.
It cannot directly access instance variables or instance methods because they belong to objects.

Exsercise 8:
class Test {

    static int x = 10;

    static {
        System.out.println("Static Block");
        x = 20;
    }

    {
        System.out.println("Instance Block");
        x++;
    }

    Test() {
        System.out.println("Constructor");
        x++;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        System.out.println(x);

        Test t1 = new Test();

        System.out.println(x);

        Test t2 = new Test();

        System.out.println(x);
    }
}
OUTPUT : The execution order in Java is:

Class Loading → Static Variable Initialization → Static Block → main() Method → Instance Initialization Block → Constructor

Static members execute only once when the class is loaded, whereas the instance block and constructor execute every time a new object is created.

